=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The NOC's report of OSPF adjacency flapping between campus core routers cannot be confirmed by any live or historical evidence in the lab. A complete multi-layer sweep was performed. At the reachability layer, all 28 tested host pairs pass with 0% loss. At the link layer, carrier-change counters on every core router interface (router7 eth2–eth5, router8 eth0/eth4, router16 bond0/eth2, mswitch0 br0) read exactly 2 — the startup-only value produced once when Kathara initializes each interface. No post-boot carrier transitions exist on any link. At the control-plane layer, all 9 FRR instances are running (vtysh and both control sockets responsive), every OSPF adjacency in the fabric is in Full state, and every router holds a consistent 18-route OSPF table. Critically, every neighbor pair shows exactly 6 FSM state changes — precisely the number accumulated during a single clean startup (Down → Init → 2-Way → ExStart → Exchange → Loading → Full). A real flap cycle would add at least 6 more transitions; none are present. OSPF timers are uniform across all interfaces (Hello 10 s, Dead 40 s), ruling out a timer-mismatch mechanism. FRR log files are empty with no neighbor-state-change events recorded, and pressure_sweep found no stress tool or flap script on any device. There is no mechanism, no historical trace, and no current symptom consistent with OSPF instability. The fault described in ticket #8117 is unconfirmed: the OSPF fabric appears to have been stable since lab initialization.

Fix: No corrective action required. If the NOC observes future transient drops, capture ospfd logs in real-time (`log file /var/log/frr/ospfd.log` in frr.conf) and re-examine carrier-change counters immediately after an event.
=== END ===